## 30. Completion and Handoff

When finished:

1. Run module-specific automated tests.
2. Run applicable type checks.
3. Report actual test results, including failures.
4. List every created and modified file.
5. Explain assessment, workstream, and reviewer ownership boundaries.
6. Document checklist template versioning.
7. Document finding and severity classifications.
8. Document evidence provenance and privacy rules.
9. Document legal-document review limitations.
10. Document measurement and remediation workflows.
11. Document report versioning and publication rules.
12. Document incomplete-evidence and assessment-quality behavior.
13. Identify dependencies on Core, Property Registry, Property Search, Property Transactions, Media, Geography, Urban Intelligence, Data Ingestion, Appointments, Service Providers, Property Management, Valuation, and Trust & Safety.
14. Identify unavailable integrations and unresolved contracts.
15. Confirm that no shared files or other employees' files were modified.
16. Commit only assigned files if explicitly authorized.
17. Do not merge into main.

Deliver a concise technical handoff report to the integration lead.

The objective is a durable, evidence-based property due-diligence backend that records inspections, documents, findings, uncertainties, and remediation history without pretending to be a legal authority, government registry, certification body, or replacement for qualified professionals.